            "lens": "review-reliability",
            "findings": [],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed both added files in full. Verify validates the feature and result name and enforces the reply size limit before any git or store call, and a test asserts that no calls are made for unsafe names.",
              "Failure paths are covered by tests: a reply that breaks the contract, a missing or corrupt stored result, a failing Read, a failing Create, an existing verdict (ErrAlreadyVerified), and an unflushed directory (reported as stored with NotFlushed set).",
              "The correction-round logic fails closed. When a blocking verdict cannot read the other stored verdicts (unparseable, missing result, or Names failing), it is refused and nothing is stored. A non-blocking verdict never consults them. Tests cover all three of these cases.",
              "Test cases cover the correction-round boundaries: same head, different base, an earlier verdict that was unverified or clear, and a current verdict that is clear or unverified.",
              "Residual, non-blocking observations: the spent-round check and the Create call are not atomic, so two concurrent blocking verifications on different heads of the same base could both pass. The check also runs before the existence check, so a duplicate blocking verdict whose round is spent reports ErrCorrectionSpent rather than ErrAlreadyVerified. Neither is exercised by realistic single-writer use, and neither makes candidate behavior unproved."
            ]
          }
        }
